[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Hey, I\'m Elijah with Inostatic and in this video, I\'m going to show you how to work with custom post types and custom data in Inostatic. If you\'re coming from another CMS like WordPress, [music] Inostatic\'s native custom post type and custom database table features will feel'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'familiar, [music] but they\'re a bit easier to work with and don\'t require any external plugins or tools. Let\'s start by creating a custom [music] post type. Within Inostatic, you can see and manage your custom post types under the'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'content [music] tab here at the top. there\'s one post type, posts, which is intended for blog posts. If we create a new post by clicking the new post button here next to the post table, we\'re able to input a title, some content including'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '{slash} commands for inserting different types of text, lists, images, and more. And on the right-hand side, we have access to the database columns or fields associated with this post type. Over'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here, we can set the slug, SEO title, SEO description, status, and featured image. But, we want our own custom post type, which is called a collection in Inostatic. To create a new collection, click the new collection button to the'

[01:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'right of the collections label in the left side panel. Give it a name. I\'ll call mine team members and take a look on the right-hand side of the modal to see all of the default fields that will'

[01:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'be added to this post type. We\'ll get a title field, slug field, a field for the body or main content of the post, as well as some SEO fields. But, I want to add a new field. So, I\'ll click add field here at the top right. We can add'

[01:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a label, determine the type of data that\'s going to be stored in this field, add a description to help people know what should go in the field, set it as required, and adjust other field behavior options, depending on the type'

[01:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of field we\'ve chosen. I\'m going to call my custom field LinkedIn profile, and for the type, I\'m going to choose URL. Then, click create field. Finally, click create at the bottom right and enter'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your password if prompted. Your custom post type is now created. Now, we\'re ready to create our first post or entry in this collection. Click new team member and you\'ll be able to create a'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'team member. You can add a title, some content, and populate the fields on the right-hand side, just like posts, including the custom LinkedIn profile field we added. But, let\'s say we want'

[02:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to add new fields to an existing post type. That\'s where the data tab comes in. The data tab gives us an overview of the actual database structure of our Instatic site. We\'re viewing the tables within our database. For instance, we'

[02:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have pages, posts, components, and layouts under system tables. But, we also have our custom tables. And here we can see our team members table. If we click on team members under custom'

[02:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tables, we can see the actual columns or fields associated with our post type. We have title, body, featured media, some SEO fields, and our LinkedIn profile field. If we want to add or change'

[02:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fields on an existing custom post type or database table, we can click the open inspector panel button at the top right to see the schema for this table. Here, I can add a field by clicking add field next to the schema label and field'

[03:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'count. This works just like adding fields when you first create your custom post type. Let\'s add one additional field called position. We\'ll make it a select field with the options manager, associate, and advisor. Now, if I go'

[03:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'back to the content tab, select my team members collection, then select one of my team members, I can see all of the fields associated with this post type, including my new position field with a'

[03:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'drop-down and the choices that I designated. This type of setup is perfect for content that you want to be able to easily create with a text editor interface, and then display on on front end of your site. But, what if you want'

[03:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to store some data in the database inside its own custom table and you don\'t need the actual post editing interface? Let\'s take a look at that next. First, let\'s go back over to the data tab. It\'s important to note that'

[04:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'while post types automatically get their own custom table, custom tables don\'t automatically create a post type. That means we can create a custom table for some data we want to store without exposing it in the content tab. For'

[04:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'instance, in static forms allow you to associate a form with a custom data table to store the submissions sent through that form. We wouldn\'t want those to appear in the content tab necessarily, but we still want the data'

[04:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to exist in our database, so they\'re stored in a custom table. Let\'s go ahead and create a new custom table now by clicking the plus symbol to the right of the custom tables label here in the left-hand panel. First, we need to give'

[04:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the table a name, and then we need to add the fields that we want to have in our schema. For this one, I\'ll leave the default name field, then I\'ll add a new field called competition, and this one can be a text field, so I\'ll click'

[04:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'create field without any other changes. Then I\'ll add another field called year received, and this one I\'ll set to the number type. You can see that since I changed the type of field this is, I now have different field behavior options,'

[05:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'such as minimum value, maximum value, step, and an integer only option. I can also choose how the number gets formatted, but the default option is fine for this one, so I\'ll click create'

[05:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'field. Now that our fields are created, I can click create to create the new database table. Now I\'m able to click add row and populate the fields that are set up in this table\'s schema. I can add a name, the type of competition, and the'

[05:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'year the award was received. I\'ll go ahead and add a few awards to this table because the next thing I want to show you is how to actually use your custom post types or content collections and'

[05:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your custom table data on the front end via the loop element. I\'m going to start on a blank page so that you can see just the loop element and kind of how it works. Let\'s click the add to canvas'

[05:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'button here near the top, search for loop, and click the loop element to drop it into our design. With a loop element selected, you can see in the right-hand panel that we have a source option. So,'

[06:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'click the drop-down and choose data rows. Next, we can choose our table. Let\'s pick team members. We can also change some other options such as how these results get ordered, how they\'re'

[06:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sorted, and how many to show at a time, as well as pagination settings. Once our loop options are set up, we can start adding elements to this loop. The elements we add will be repeated for each item returned in the loop and act'

[06:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'as a sort of template. So, let\'s start with a container. After we have our container added, which you can see is repeated three times because we have three team members in our database, let\'s drop in an image and three text'

[06:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'elements. To insert the actual data from our team members posts, select the image element, hover over the image field, and click the bind to data field button. Here, we can choose the featured image.'

[07:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Once that\'s selected, we can now see the featured image for all three of our team members here in the loop. Next, select the text element, hover over the text field label, and click the insert data token button. I\'ll choose title to'

[07:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'insert the team member\'s name, and note that the data tokens are inserted into the text fields via a template tag with curly braces. These can actually be concatenated with other text, as you can see here, but in this case, we don\'t'

[07:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want the original text, so we\'ll just remove that default text from the field. Let\'s insert another data token in this other text element, but this time, we\'ll put the body of the post, which will be the kind of short bio for the team member. Finally, in the third text'

[07:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'element, let\'s drop in their position. Now, we need to style it up. So, first I\'ll select the loop element itself, add a class, and set its layout to a three-column grid with one of our CSS'

[07:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'variables as the gap value. Next, I\'ll select the image since they\'re not all the same aspect ratio, add a class, and set them to an aspect ratio of 1:1 under the size settings. Then, under'

[08:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'background, I\'ll set the object fit to cover. Finally, I can add a class to the container within the loop and give it a gap as well. It\'s no masterpiece, and we could style it a lot more to make it'

[08:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'look better, but this sufficiently demonstrates how easy it is to output custom post types via the loop element. And luckily, displaying data from custom database tables works exactly the same. Let\'s go ahead and build a loop for our'

[08:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'awards table data. Just like before, we\'ll drop in a loop element, then a container, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; then a few text elements, then we\'ll wire up the data to each element using the insert [music] data token button, then tweak the styling of it. Whether'

[08:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'re dealing with custom post types or [music] custom database tables, Instat makes it easy to not only create the data structure you need, [music] but also display it on the front end using its powerful loop capabilities.'

